we are more than a week now into the  governor's stay-at-home 2.0 advisory and  as you just heard there  his office is looking at ways to stop  the spread of coronavirus  if things don't improve while we don't  know what that will look like  it is a cause for concern among some  small businesses  sasha loft is live tonight with how  they're feeling  sasha  denise and brian i spoke to the owner of  a small local boutique tonight  she says this year has been devastating  with local restrictions related to cov19  and she says the idea of another  shutdown would just  really be tough especially for her and  those everyone else who relies on the  local economy  it has been horrible jessica boone  shares the struggle she's faced as a  small business owner  in the age of covid19 there are days  when we don't have a single person walk  through the doors  and it's scary she's managed to keep her  west valley boutique in business so far  but with talks of another potential  shutdown she's concerned  for the future it's hard to hear that  i'm not essential  when it's my livelihood this is how it  helps support my family in the next  two weeks the governor has encouraged  nevadans to stay home  citing a resurgence in coronavirus case  numbers  e-7 health ceo dr jonathan bhaktari says  his team  has seen a concerning uptick we've been  seeing both  an uptick in testing and we've been  seeing an uptick in positive results  especially among younger people he says  more activity leads to more risk so it's  crucial  for us all to take precautions the more  parties you go to the more people you  interact with  it's strictly a numbers game you're  going to be at more risk and jessica  encourages  everyone to stay healthy so she can keep  her doors  safely open it's hard to see the big  places who  will definitely live through this and so  many of our  local small businesses might not make it  jessica also has an online component to  her store she said she will fall back on  that if she absolutely has to but of  course  relies on that money from in-store  purchases  she says she does limit capacity because  of kovid 19 of course  encourage sanitization and hand washing  she sanitizes her store multiple times a  day  and requires masks for now reporting  live sasha loftis  8 news now
 

